      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: WEATHERING"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Weathering is an in-situ (static) process involving the disintegration and decomposition of rocks without transportation.",
        "Erosion is a dynamic process involving the active wearing away and transportation of rock materials by mobile agents.",
        "The four major agents of erosion and deposition are running water (rivers), wind, glaciers, and marine waves.",
        "Deposition occurs when the kinetic energy or transport capacity of erosional agents decreases, building new landforms.",
        "Understanding weathering, erosion, and deposition is critical for analyzing soil formation, mineral enrichment, and landscape evolution."
      ],
      "id": "0o3sc7z"
    },
